Comments: [Evaluator: Christian Iliadis; Date: 03/16/2023]
1.  Ercm= -3.1 keV: Gg and Ga from Mak et al. 1980; C2S from Landre et al. 1989.
2.  Ercm= -1.6 keV: Gg and Ga from Mak et al. 1980; C2S upper limit from transfer work of Landre et al. 1989; the much smaller value from the DC 
    work of Rolfs & Rodney 1975 (C2S<0.0003) is not trustworthy considering that for the Ercm= -3.1 keV level the transfer work yields C2S=0.12+-0.04, 
    while the DC work gives C2S<0.002. Value of the mean reduced width, needed for the Porter-Thomas distribution (0.001, with factor 5 uncertainty) 
    is estimated from Pogrebnyak et al. 2013. For more information, see discussion in Buckner et al. 2015.
3.  Ercm=556 keV and 677 keV: partial widths from R-matrix analysis of Kontos et al. 2012, but their tiny uncertainties on Gg [about 1%] have 
    been inflated to match uncertainties of their measured resonance strengths; their partial widths agree with their directly measured (p,g) 
    resonance strengths.
4.  Ercm=65 keV: partial widths from measured (p,a) [Bayesian weighted average from Blackmon et al. 1995; Hannam and Thompson 1999; Bruno et al. 2016] 
    and (a,g) resonance strengths [Parker 1968; Rolfs et al. 1973; Berka et al. 1977; Becker et al. 1982], and from Ga [14N(a,a) R-matrix analysis of 
    Mak et al. 1980].
5.  Ercm=183 keV: partial widths are computed from measured (p,g), (p,a), and (a,g) strengths. Although results are correlated, tests have shown that
    this does not impact the (p,a) resonance strength.
6.  Unless noted otherwise: Gp and Ga adopted from Tab. 3 of Kieser et al. 1979, while Gg is calculated from our adopted (p,g) resonance strengths.
7.  If Gg is unknown: an arbitrary value of Gg=0.1+-0.001 eV is used; inconsequential for total rate.


